WALK = [(0, None, [('first_guest', 'закуску'), "'Taste this appetizer, gentlemen.' ", ' is the little dish you eat alongside a drink, and the word for gentlemen is an old word that came back into use. It sounds slightly grand, which suits the caviar.']), (1, None, [('second_guest', 'Слушайте'), "'Listen, this is wonderful caviar!' Russians begin with ", ' when they have something to say. The word for wonderful comes from the word for miracle. The guest is warming up.']), (2, None, [('first_guest', 'чувствуется'), "'You can immediately sense that it is natural.' The Russian phrase translates to at once it is felt. The self-directed ending of ", ' makes the feeling come by itself. The word for natural is similar to English. The guest trusts his palate.']), (3, None, [('second_guest', 'ел'), "'Yes, I only ever ate such caviar in Astrakhan.' Astrakhan is a southern river city known for its caviar, so this is high praise. ", ' means ate. Word for word it is such only in Astrakhan ate.']), (4, None, [('first_guest', 'встретишь'), "'In Moscow you rarely encounter such quality.' ", ' means you will meet, with you meaning anyone. The word for rarely is used here. The city name changes its ending after the connecting word for in.']), (5, None, [('second_guest', 'вкусно'), "'Yes, delicious, very delicious!' ", ' without the descriptive word ending means it is tasty, and Russians shout it at the table. The repetition is genuine enthusiasm. The second guest cannot get enough.']), (6, None, [('first_guest', 'Шеф-повар'), "'The head chef of this restaurant is a true master.' ", ' joins chef and cook into a title. The word for master is the same as the Russian word for the repairmen in the stove story. The guest praises the chef.']), (7, None, [('second_guest', 'подходит'), "'And the fresh bread goes with it perfectly.' ", " means suits, from the walking-up action word as in Fedya's command to approach. The phrase for fresh bread is followed by the word for excellently."]), (8, None, [('first_guest', 'Обязательно'), "'We will definitely order another portion.' ", " means without fail, heard in Max's promise of a big wedding. The action word means we will order, from the root for order. The word for portion is similar to English."]), (9, None, [('second_guest', 'приходить'), "'I am ready to come here every day.' ", ' means to come, in the form for coming again and again. The word for ready is followed by the phrase for every day.']), (10, None, [('first_guest', 'Передайте'), "'Pass on our sincere gratitude to the chef.' ", ' means pass on, just like when Kostya said he would pass on your words. The phrase for sincere gratitude is a longer, formal thanks. The word for to the chef has a directional ending.']), (11, None, [('second_guest', 'удался'), "'This evening was a glorious success!' The Russian idiom translates as succeeded to glory, an idiom for went wonderfully. ", ' grows from the root for luck. The guest closes the scene with a flourish.'])]